<p>You reported hail damage, the adjuster came out, and a few weeks later a letter arrived saying the claim is denied. If you are trying to figure out <strong>what to do if your hail claim was denied in Texas</strong>, the first step is not to argue. It is to understand exactly what the insurer decided and why. A denial letter is a statement of position, and positions can be tested with better information.</p>

<p>This guide gives Dallas-area homeowners a practical, step-by-step plan: how to read the letter, rebuild the evidence, tie the damage to a specific storm, ask for a second look, and know when to bring in a Dallas Public Adjuster, TDI, or an attorney. It does not promise a different result. Coverage always depends on your policy and the facts. But a careful response gives you the best chance of a fair review.</p>

<h2>Why hail claims get denied in Texas</h2>

<p>Hail is routine in North Texas. NOAA’s Storm Events Database lists 841 hail reports for Dallas County since 1955, and busy years can bring dozens of reports, with 2011, 2012, and 2023 all topping 50. After a major storm, insurers receive a wave of claims at once, inspections can be fast, and borderline damage is easy to dismiss. Here are the reasons that show up most often, and what usually answers each one:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Denial reason</th><th>What the insurer is saying</th><th>Evidence that can answer it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>No hail damage found</td><td>The adjuster did not see storm damage</td><td>Close-up photos of marked impacts on each slope, soft-metal dents, a qualified roofer’s or engineer’s report</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wear and tear or age</td><td>Damage is from deterioration, not a storm</td><td>Photos showing random impact patterns, fresh fractures, dated pre-storm photos of the roof</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pre-existing or prior-storm damage</td><td>The damage happened before your policy period or before this storm</td><td>NOAA reports for the date, prior inspection records, purchase inspection, earlier photos</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late notice</td><td>You reported too long after the storm</td><td>Timeline showing when you discovered the damage and reported it; policy notice terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Excluded or cosmetic damage</td><td>The policy does not cover this type of damage</td><td>The exact exclusion wording, evidence the damage affects function (for example, a broken seal or punctured panel)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Below the deductible</td><td>Covered damage costs less than your wind/hail deductible</td><td>A complete line-item estimate that includes missed items</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>TDI points out that the most common reason for any home claim denial is that the policy simply does not cover that type of damage. With hail, though, many disputes are really about evidence: whether the damage exists, whether it came from a storm, and whether it came from a storm during your policy period.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Read the denial letter line by line</h2>

<p>Read it slowly, with your policy next to it. Note:</p>
<ul>
<li>The specific reason given and any policy sections quoted</li>
<li>The date of loss the insurer used, and whether it matches the storm you reported</li>
<li>Which parts of the property were inspected and which were not</li>
<li>Whether the denial is total or partial (for example, siding paid but roof denied)</li>
<li>Any deadlines or instructions for submitting more information</li>
</ul>

<p>If the letter is vague, write back and ask for the specific policy language, the inspection report, and the photos the decision relied on. TDI confirms that insurers must explain a denial in writing, and asking for the underlying documents is reasonable.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Organize your claim file</h2>

<p>Gather everything in one place, paper or digital: the policy and declarations page, the claim number, every letter and email, the insurer’s estimate if there is one, your photos, contractor estimates, and receipts for tarping or emergency repairs. Start a simple log with the date, person, and summary of every call. When you send something to the insurer, keep a copy and note how and when it was sent.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/What-to-Do-If-Your-Hail-Claim-Was-Denied-in-Texas-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Two men at a desk reviewing printed roof damage photos with roof images on a monitor" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Organizing photos, estimates, and letters makes it easier to answer the specific reason given for a denial.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Step 3: Re-document the damage</h2>

<p>If the first round of photos did not persuade the insurer, the next round needs to be clearer and more systematic. Good documentation for a denied claim usually includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Wide shots of each roof slope and each side of the house, so the reader knows where every close-up came from</li>
<li>Close-ups of individual impacts, circled in chalk, with a tape measure or coin for scale</li>
<li>Soft metals: gutters, downspouts, turtle vents, pipe jacks, flashing, and the AC condenser</li>
<li>Collateral damage such as screens, garage doors, fences, painted trim, and outdoor furniture</li>
<li>Interior water stains and attic decking, if leaks have started</li>
</ul>

<p>Our guide on <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</a> and the <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walk through this in detail.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Tie the damage to a specific storm</h2>

<p>When an insurer says the damage is old or from another event, storm data matters. NOAA’s <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storm Events Database</a> records hail reports by date and location. Look for reports near your address on or around the date you reported. You can use our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> to search Dallas County events quickly.</p>

<p>This is especially useful for storms outside the usual spring peak. Most Dallas County hail falls in April and May, so a fall storm can get less attention. For example, NOAA logged hail up to 2.25 inches in <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1142401" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richardson</a> and <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1142399" target="_blank" rel="noopener">northern Garland</a> on September 24, 2023. More recently, hail up to about 3 inches was reported around <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1326034" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dallas Baptist University</a> on April 25, 2026. A report like that does not prove your roof was damaged, but it does show that damaging hail fell nearby on that date.</p>

<p>If you have dated photos of the roof from before the storm, such as from a home purchase inspection or a real estate listing, include them. Before-and-after images are among the most persuasive evidence on a wear-and-tear dispute.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Step 5: Get a qualified second opinion</h2>

<p>An independent inspection report carries more weight than a general opinion. Look for a report from a reputable roofer, a licensed engineer, or a public adjuster that:</p>
<ul>
<li>Identifies hail impacts by slope and component, with photos</li>
<li>Explains why the damage is storm-related rather than age or manufacturing defects</li>
<li>Notes the roof type, approximate age, and condition before the storm if known</li>
<li>Includes a line-item repair or replacement estimate</li>
</ul>

<p>Be cautious with contractors who promise to “get your roof approved” or offer to cover your deductible. TDI warns that waiving a homeowner’s deductible is illegal in Texas, and Texas law does not allow contractors to act as your public adjuster.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/What-to-Do-If-Your-Hail-Claim-Was-Denied-in-Texas-2-1024x683.webp" alt="Inspector in a cap kneeling on a damaged shingle roof and writing on a clipboard" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A slope-by-slope inspection with marked, photographed impacts gives the insurer specific findings to review.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Step 6: Respond in writing and request a reinspection</h2>

<p>Send a clear, professional letter or email to your adjuster. Keep it factual:</p>
<ol>
<li>Identify the claim number, date of loss, and the denial date.</li>
<li>State which finding you disagree with and why, in a sentence or two.</li>
<li>List the new evidence you are attaching.</li>
<li>Ask for a reinspection, and ask that your contractor or representative be allowed to attend.</li>
<li>Ask the insurer to respond in writing.</li>
</ol>

<p>At the reinspection, walk the property with the adjuster, show each marked area, and take your own notes and photos. If you want, you can ask whether a different adjuster can be assigned, though the insurer decides that.</p>

<h2>Step 7: Know your escalation options</h2>

<h3>Appraisal</h3>
<p>Most Texas property policies include an appraisal clause. Either side can demand appraisal to resolve a disagreement over the <em>amount</em> of loss. Each side picks an appraiser, the two appraisers choose an umpire, and an award agreed to by any two is binding on the amount, subject to the policy language. Appraisal does not decide coverage, so it fits partial denials and underpayments better than full coverage denials. See our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page for more.</p>

<h3>A TDI complaint</h3>
<p>If the insurer is not responding or you believe the claim was handled unfairly, you can <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/get-help-with-an-insurance-complaint.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">file a complaint with the Texas Department of Insurance</a>. TDI can ask the company to look at the claim again. Its consumer help line is 800-252-3439.</p>

<h3>An attorney</h3>
<p>For lawsuits, bad-faith questions, or deadlines to sue, talk to a Texas attorney. Weather-related property claims are subject to Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542A, which requires written notice at least 61 days before filing suit, and many policies have their own contractual limitation periods. A public adjuster cannot give legal advice.</p>

<h2>Partial denials and “paid but not enough” claims</h2>

<p>Many claims are not fully denied. The insurer pays for gutters and screens but not the roof, or approves a repair on two slopes when your roofer says the roof needs replacement. Treat these the same way: identify each disputed item, document it, and submit a supplement or reconsideration request. If the remaining dispute is about price or quantity, appraisal may be a fit. Our page on <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> and our guide to <a href="/hail-damage-claim-supplements-what-carriers-miss-in-their-scope/">hail damage claim supplements</a> explain these options.</p>

<h2>Deadlines to keep in mind</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Insurer response times.</strong> Under Texas’s Prompt Payment of Claims law, insurers generally must acknowledge a claim within 15 days, and decide within 15 business days after receiving everything they requested, with up to 45 more days if they notify you they need more time. Deadlines can extend 15 days after a declared weather catastrophe.</li>
<li><strong>Policy deadlines.</strong> Many policies require repairs within a set time to collect recoverable depreciation, and some set limits on reporting wind and hail claims.</li>
<li><strong>Lawsuit deadlines.</strong> Often two years and one day from when the claim accrued under many Texas policies. Confirm with an attorney.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/What-to-Do-If-Your-Hail-Claim-Was-Denied-in-Texas-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster explaining notes on a clipboard to a couple standing outside their home" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Understanding the timeline and your options helps you respond before important deadlines pass.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Mistakes that hurt a denied claim</h2>
<ul>
<li>Giving up after the first letter without asking for the reasons and documents</li>
<li>Sending emotional complaints instead of specific, evidence-based responses</li>
<li>Making permanent repairs before the insurer has a chance to reinspect</li>
<li>Letting a contractor communicate with the insurer “for you” in ways Texas law does not allow</li>
<li>Missing a policy or legal deadline while waiting for an answer</li>
</ul>

<h2>Preventing the next denial</h2>
<p>Take dated photos of your roof every spring before storm season, keep your purchase and roof inspection reports, report hail damage promptly, and read your policy at every renewal for changes to roof coverage, deductibles, or cosmetic exclusions. Those small habits make the next claim much easier to support.</p>

<h2>When a Dallas Public Adjuster can help</h2>

<p>A public adjuster is licensed by TDI to represent policyholders, not insurers. On a denied hail claim, that usually means reviewing the policy and the denial, re-inspecting and documenting the property, preparing a line-item estimate, and presenting the claim to the insurer. Whether the decision changes depends on the policy and the evidence; no one can promise a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<p>If you are weighing your options, our comparison of <a href="/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster-for-hail-claims-in-texas/">a public adjuster vs. an insurance adjuster</a> explains the difference, and our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> service serves Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities.</p>
